window.POSTS = [{"id":"p-firing","title":"Firing Developers Won’t Make Software Cheaper","date":"2025-06-21","url":"/blog/2025/06/21/firing-developers-wont-make-software-cheaper/","tags":["AI","software"],"excerpt":"Let’s stop asking, “When can I fire my developers?” Let’s ask, “What can we build so we don’t need as many developers in the first place?”","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["inferal"],"minutes":3},{"id":"p-pgext25","title":"Postgres Extensions Day 2025 Montreal: Registration and Call for Speakers","date":"2025-02-26","url":"/blog/2025/02/26/postgres-extensions-day-2025-montreal-registration-and-call-for-speakers/","tags":["Postgres"],"excerpt":"On May 12th, 2025, Postgres Extension Developers Coalition (PGEDC) will host Postgres Extensions Day 2025 in Montreal, QC.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pgext"],"minutes":1},{"id":"p-3x","title":"Repeating Yourself Thrice Doesn’t Turn You Into a 3x Developer","date":"2023-07-30","url":"/blog/2023/07/30/repeating-yourself-thrice-doesnt-turn-you-into-a-3x-developer/","tags":["Omnigres","architecture"],"excerpt":"Three is a magic number. This is the number of things we can keep in our minds without losing focus. So, logically, a three-tier software architecture (database, backend and frontend) is a great model.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":7},{"id":"p-d3x","title":"Omnigres Developer Experience","date":"2023-07-20","url":"/blog/2023/07/20/omnigres-developer-experience/","tags":["Omnigres","D3X"],"excerpt":"Software developer's job is not an easy one; anything that makes it less frustrating and makes developers more productive is highly sought for. This is why the most successful developer tools are usually the ones that have an amazing developer experience.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":6},{"id":"p-regr","title":"Structured Postgres Regression Tests","date":"2023-04-23","url":"/blog/2023/04/23/structured-postgres-regression-tests/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres"],"excerpt":"I've been using pg_regress tests for a while. It's generally a great way to ensure the behavior of your Postgres code works and continues working as expected. However, as my tests became larger, I started getting lost in them; and there are limits as to what you can test by having a psql session.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":3},{"id":"p-lim","title":"Avoiding Postgres Extensions Limitations","date":"2023-04-10","url":"/blog/2023/04/10/avoiding-postgres-extensions-limitations/","tags":["Postgres","tips"],"excerpt":"Postgres extensions are great. They enable fantastic use cases and bring new capabilities to one of the most loved open source databases. But there are edges in some of its features and this can be heard in conversations: limitations of the upgrade system, lack of parameterized initialization, search path/OID resolution issues, hard-wired dependency on.control files, schema droppage, etc.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":3},{"id":"p-sum","title":"Sum Types in Postgres","date":"2023-04-09","url":"/blog/2023/04/09/sum-types-in-postgres/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres","types"],"excerpt":"At times, representing the variety of types a value can take through multiple tables can taxing, both in terms of development complexity as well as, potentially, performance. You must just need a value to be of any of the given variants. I've set out to build a generalized mechanism for defining these.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":3},{"id":"p-plrust","title":"PL/Rust Just Shipped: Easy Way to Try It Out","date":"2023-04-05","url":"/blog/2023/04/05/plrust-just-shipped-easy-way-to-try-it-out/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres","Rust","Docker","containers"],"excerpt":"As a Rust enthusiast and a contributor to a sister project I am stoked about the release of PL/Rust 1.0.0 that was just announced.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":2},{"id":"p-game","title":"Make Postgres an Application Server, Gamified","date":"2023-04-04","url":"/blog/2023/04/04/make-postgres-an-application-server-gamified/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres","contest"],"excerpt":"Have you ever wondered if Postgres can be a fully self-sufficient platform for your application? Learn how to make it become an application server and win some prizes, too!","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":4},{"id":"p-http","title":"What happens if you put HTTP server inside Postgres?","date":"2023-02-16","url":"/blog/2023/02/16/what-happens-if-you-put-http-server-inside-postgres/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres","performance"],"excerpt":"Benchmarks and performance claims are attention-grabbers, but that's not what drew me to work on Omnigres. When I first built a prototype of its HTTP server, I didn't foresee the desire to share the numbers. As we all know, getting benchmarks right is hard, and everybody's mileage may vary. But I'll show you some numbers here anyway. It'll be great to validate or invalidate my findings!","listed":true,"featured":true,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":6},{"id":"p-rust","title":"Why not Rust for Omnigres?","date":"2023-01-07","url":"/blog/2023/01/07/why-not-rust-for-omnigres/","tags":["Postgres","Omnigres","Rust","C"],"excerpt":"Omnigres is a new project to turn Postgres into a complete development and production deployment platform. I've started it to reflect on the complexity and inefficiencies plaguing modern business software development.","listed":true,"featured":true,"related":["omnigres"],"minutes":5},{"id":"p-dear-oss","title":"Dear Open Source Projects…","date":"2018-03-15","url":"/blog/2018/03/15/dear-open-source-projects/","tags":["open source"],"excerpt":"By using a closed source, proprietary product you are incentivizing others to create more closed source, proprietary products.","listed":true,"featured":false,"minutes":6},{"id":"p-covers","title":"Under the Covers of Code","date":"2018-01-31","url":"/blog/2018/01/31/under-the-covers-of-code/","tags":["Git","practices"],"excerpt":"I remember that about 20 years ago, when I was just a bit more naive and a lot more confident in my opinions, I thought that code is the ultimate source of truth. Documentation was secondary to me.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["p-c4"],"minutes":5},{"id":"p-c4","title":"Solving Problems, One Commit at a Time","date":"2017-09-04","url":"/blog/2017/09/04/solving-problems-one-commit-at-a-time/","tags":["Git","C4","practices"],"excerpt":"If you have been following some of my projects in the past year or so, you might have noticed that the my commit messages changed considerably and they no longer look typical. Take a look at this one:","listed":true,"featured":true,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":4},{"id":"p-abstractions","title":"Removing Obscuring Abstractions","date":"2017-06-09","url":"/blog/2017/06/09/removing-obscuring-abstractions/","tags":["PumpkinDB","databases"],"excerpt":"Well over a decade ago Chuck Moore mentioned an idea of “unnecessary abstractions”, abstractions that obscure the nature of the underlying concept.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":6},{"id":"p-pumpkins","title":"Your Functional Programs Are Imperative Pumpkins","date":"2016-10-30","url":"/blog/2016/10/30/your-functional-programs-are-imperative-pumpkins/","tags":["event sourcing","databases","functional programming"],"excerpt":"Earlier this year, on a rainy night in Bangkok, I was attending a regular Hack Night event, and I just had to jump into somebody’s else conversation without an invitation.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":4},{"id":"p-no-user","title":"There Is No “User”","date":"2016-09-26","url":"/blog/2016/09/26/there-is-no-user/","tags":["event sourcing","databases"],"excerpt":"We all have had some of our lightbulb moments happen at the wrong time. This one happened to me at the very beginning of a project, the time when you need to get stuff out of the door as quickly as possible to test the assumptions and hypotheses.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":4},{"id":"p-lazy-es","title":"Lazy Event Sourcing","date":"2016-04-13","url":"/blog/2016/04/13/lazy-event-sourcing/","tags":["event sourcing","databases"],"excerpt":"If you read Fowler’s article on event sourcing or learned about it through many other sources, there’s one particular mental picture of this approach that you might have affixed to your brain.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":3},{"id":"p-team-play","title":"The Team Play Myth","date":"2014-09-07","url":"/blog/2014/09/07/the-team-play-myth/","tags":["practices","teamwork"],"excerpt":"After I wrote the original piece on Team Uncommunication, I’ve been thinking a lot on how this can be practically implemented.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["p-canoe"],"minutes":2},{"id":"p-canoe","title":"Team Uncommunication","date":"2014-04-21","url":"/blog/2014/04/21/team-uncommunication/","tags":["practices","teamwork"],"excerpt":"Last year, my friends and I spent a few days by a lake on Vancouver Island. The house we were renting came with two canoes and, naturally, one of my friends and I ended up paddling one. Being quite new to canoes, we were at first struggling to keep the thing straight and move swiftly.","listed":true,"featured":true,"minutes":2},{"id":"p-save","title":"Is “Save” Obsolete?","date":"2008-07-13","url":"/blog/2008/07/13/is-save-obsolete/","tags":["databases","StrokeDB"],"excerpt":"Leaving my hangover apart, I want to talk about some recent \"realization\" I had. I am not sure it is necessarily a good idea, but it might be worth some investigation. So here we go.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":2},{"id":"p-no-database","title":"We Don’t Need a “Database”","date":"2008-05-16","url":"/blog/2008/05/16/we-dont-need-a-database/","tags":["databases","StrokeDB"],"excerpt":"I’ve been trying to formulate what StrokeDB is recently. And here is my summary: StrokeDB is not a database; it is a programming environment on top of Ruby.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":2},{"id":"p-docdb-fud","title":"Top 10 Reasons to Avoid Document Databases FUD","date":"2008-04-26","url":"/blog/2008/04/26/top-10-reasons-to-avoid-document-databases-fud/","tags":["databases","StrokeDB"],"excerpt":"First of all I’d like to note that the below answers are not about SimpleDB but rather to prevent FUD about document-based databases.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":5},{"id":"p-couchdb","title":"What Is Missing in CouchDB Approach","date":"2007-11-22","url":"/blog/2007/11/22/what-is-missing-in-couchdb-approach/","tags":["databases","CouchDB"],"excerpt":"CouchDb approach is missing one simple idea — object’s slot (or field) should be an object too.","listed":true,"featured":false,"related":["pumpkindb"],"minutes":1}];
